course: euphemisms
lesson: 13
title: Soft words for hard things: relations and family
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Families are complicated, and people use soft words so they do not have to explain quarrels, divorces or who lives with whom.
explain: Some soft words are kind («blended family»), some are jokey («better half»), some are official («irreconcilable differences»), and some old ones are unkind («on the shelf»).
explain: Often the soft phrase hides a sad or messy story: «we're not close» can mean «we don't speak to each other».
explain: Vivid example: a star posts «it's complicated» about a relationship, which means nobody, maybe not even the two of them, knows if they are still together.
fact: "Single-parent family" means a family where one parent raises the children.
fact: "Blended family" means a family with a stepparent and stepchildren.
fact: "Other half" means husband, wife or partner (everyday, UK).
fact: "Better half" means husband or wife (jokey).
fact: "Significant other" means partner (polite, US).
fact: "Plus-one" means the guest or partner you are allowed to bring to an event.
fact: "Estranged" means not in contact any more after a quarrel.
fact: "We're not close" often means family members do not get on or rarely speak.
fact: "Black sheep" means the family member the others are ashamed of.
fact: "Living in sin" is an old jokey phrase for living together without being married.
fact: "Common-law wife" means a woman a man lives with as a wife without marrying her.
fact: "Gentleman friend" means an older woman's boyfriend (old-fashioned).
fact: "Lady friend" means a man's girlfriend (old-fashioned).
fact: "On a break" means a couple has split up for a while and may get back together.
fact: "It's complicated" means a relationship is messy or uncertain.
fact: "Taking some time apart" means separating, and often ends in a break-up.
fact: "Irreconcilable differences" is the official reason given for a divorce (US).
fact: "On the shelf" is an old, unkind phrase for someone seen as too old to marry (UK).
fact: "Footloose and fancy-free" means single and free of ties.
fact: "Family issues" means serious trouble or quarrels in a family.
exercise: What is a "single-parent family"? | A family where one parent raises the children | A family with only one child and no cousins nearby | A family where both parents are single | A family that lives alone in the country
exercise: Jake has a stepmum and two stepbrothers. What kind of family is that? | A blended family | A single-parent family | A broken family | A foster home
exercise: Dad says, "I'll ask the other half." Who will he ask? | His wife or partner | His twin brother | The other team | His half-brother
exercise: Uncle Tom says, "My better half says no cake." Who said no? | His wife | His doctor | His sister | His boss at work
exercise: An American invitation says, "Bring your significant other." Who should come? | Your partner | Your most important client | Your best schoolfriend | Your eldest child
exercise: The wedding card says, "You and a plus-one." What does that mean? | You may bring one guest or partner | You must bring a present | You get one extra slice of cake | You may come one day later
exercise: The news says the actor is "estranged from his father." What does that mean? | They are not in contact after a quarrel | His father lives far away in another country | His father is a stranger | His father is very strange
exercise: Asked about her brother, Mum says, "We're not close." What does she probably mean? | They do not get on or rarely speak | They live far away from each other | They are not twins | They are not related
exercise: "Cousin Ned is the black sheep of the family." What is Ned? | The relative the family is ashamed of | The one with the darkest, curliest hair of them all | The one who keeps sheep | The youngest of the family
exercise: Great-Gran grumbled that the young couple were "living in sin." What did she mean? | They lived together without being married | They broke the law | They were very naughty and rude to old people | They lived in a bad area
exercise: An old court paper mentions his "common-law wife." Who is she? | A woman he lives with as a wife but never married | A wife chosen for him by a judge in a court of law | A lawyer he married | His first wife from long ago
exercise: Great-Aunt May, aged 70, has a "gentleman friend." Who is he? | Her boyfriend | Her old butler | Her family doctor | Her neighbour's son
exercise: Grandpa has a "lady friend" who comes to Sunday lunch. Who is she? | His girlfriend | His cleaner | His daughter | His nurse
exercise: "Ross and Rachel are on a break." What does it mean? | They have split up for now | They are having a cup of tea | They are on holiday together | They broke something
exercise: "Are you with Ben?" "It's complicated." What does the answer mean? | The relationship is messy or uncertain | Ben is hard to understand when he talks fast | The question is too hard | They are doing hard homework
exercise: "We're taking some time apart." What is happening to the couple? | They are separating and may break up | They are working different shifts | They are going on separate trips | They are counting the hours
exercise: The divorce papers say "irreconcilable differences." What does that mean? | The reason they are divorcing | They just have different hobbies | Their bank accounts do not match | They only ever argue about money
exercise: An old novel calls a woman of 30 "on the shelf." What is the old, unkind meaning? | Seen as too old to marry | Very tidy | Waiting in a shop | Good at reaching high places
exercise: Uncle Bob says he is "footloose and fancy-free." What is he? | Single with no ties | A good dancer | Out of work | Wearing no shoes
exercise: The coach says Mia missed training because of "family issues." What does it mean? | There is serious trouble at home | Her family went on holiday | She forgot her kit | Her family subscribes to magazines
test: Divorce announcement: "Citing irreconcilable differences, the couple will divorce." Why are they divorcing? | They say they can no longer get on | They have different jobs in different towns | They want to save money | They are moving countries
test: Gossip: "His lady friend? Oh, she's been round every day since Easter." Who is she? | His girlfriend | His sister | His cleaner | His carer
test: Uncle at a barbecue: "Ask the boss, mate", pointing at his wife. What is "the boss" here? | His wife | His manager | The cook at the grill | His old mum
test: News: "The prince, who has been estranged from his family, flew home alone." What is true? | He and his family are not in contact | He is a stranger to all of them now | He was lost | He was adopted
test: Teen: "nah he's footloose and fancy-free now, dumped her last week." What does it tell you? | He is single now | He is dancing barefoot | He is away on holiday | He lost his shoes
test: Wedding reply card: "Name: ___ Plus-one: ___". What goes in the second blank? | The guest you are bringing | Your age, with one year added | A second gift | A second address
test: Aunt: "Your cousin? Black sheep, love. Don't ask." What is the aunt saying? | The cousin is the family's shame | The cousin has dark hair | The cousin works on a sheep farm up north | The cousin is the oldest
test: Friend: "Mum and Dad are taking some time apart. Dad's at Gran's." What is likely happening? | The parents are separating | Dad is on holiday at the seaside | Gran is ill | They are redecorating
test: Status update: "Relationship: it's complicated." What is the person saying? | The relationship is unclear or messy | They have no relationship at all | They are married and happy | They are learning maths
test: Old aunt, rough: "Thirty and still on the shelf, poor thing." What does she mean (unkindly)? | She thinks the woman is too old to marry | She thinks the woman is tidy | The woman works in a library | The woman cannot reach the shelf
test: Kid: "We're a blended family, I got 2 stepsisters." What is true? | There is a stepparent with children | The family likes making fruit smoothies | The family is mixed-race | The sisters are twins
test: Gossip: "Them two? On a break again. Back together by Friday, you'll see." What is the gossip saying? | They have split up for now but may get back together | They are on holiday together at the seaside this week | They are resting at work | They broke a plate
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the expression "significant other" as employed in formal American correspondence. | A person's romantic partner | A person's principal business associate | A person's eldest son or daughter | A person of considerable public importance
exam: It is stated that an individual's family is a "single-parent family". Ascertain which of the following is thereby established. | The children are raised by one parent | The household comprises a single child | Neither parent has contracted marriage | The family resides in isolation
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the phrase "living in sin" in former times. | Cohabitation without the solemnisation of marriage | Residence in a disreputable district of a town or city | Conduct in breach of criminal law | Habitual misbehaviour of children
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a remark by a parent that she and her sibling are "not close"? | The siblings have a poor or distant relationship | The siblings reside at a considerable distance | The siblings are not related by blood | The siblings were not born as twins
